a second injury on top of the first."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The myth that does the most damage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pain during styling is widely treated as proof the work was done properly — that the braids will last, that the parting will stay clean. It is nothing of the sort. Pain is the follicle reporting a load it cannot carry."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Saying so in the chair is awkward, and plenty of people don't. But a style loosened at the front while it is being put in costs nothing, and it is far cheaper than treatment years later."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The same applies to adhesives along the hairline, edge gels brushed in with a hard-bristled brush every morning, and any style that has to be slept in without being loosened."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can traction alopecia grow back?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"While the follicle is alive, yes — if the pulling stops. Hair grows slowly, so this is judged over months. Nobody can give you a timeline before looking at your scalp, and anyone who offers one before examining you is guessing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once the follicle has been replaced by fibrous tissue, no. That is scarring alopecia, and it is permanent in the literal sense: there is no longer a structure there to regrow from. Products cannot reverse it and neither can time."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most people who come in are somewhere between the two, with some areas still viable and some not. Working out which is which is the point of an assessment, and it is not something you can do reliably in a mirror."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What actually helps"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Taking the tension off comes first, and nothing else works without it. In practice that means larger sections rather than fine ones at the hairline, lighter extensions, shorter wear time, and a genuine rest period between styles instead of a same-day changeover."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It also means treating any inflammation on the scalp. A flaking, itchy or bump-prone scalp is a worse environment for a follicle already under stress, and that is treatable on its own."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beyond that, the clinic's options depend on what the assessment finds. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/treatments/hair-transplant-nigeria","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Our hair restoration service"},{"type":"text","text":" covers both the non-surgical treatments used while follicles are still viable and the surgical route for areas that have scarred:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PRP hair restoration — ₦150,000"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PRF hair restoration — ₦250,000"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Exosome hair treatment — ₦200,000"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dandruff and seborrhoea treatment — ₦120,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These are current as of July 2026, and a consultation confirms what applies to you. PRP and PRF act on follicles that are shrinking rather than gone, which is exactly why the early window is worth catching — the same treatment does nothing for scarred skin."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When it has gone too far"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For areas that have scarred, a hair transplant is the only way to put hair back. That is a real option and the clinic performs it, but scarred scalp carries a poorer blood supply than healthy scalp, so candidacy is assessed rather than assumed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"There is also a sequencing problem worth understanding. Transplanting into a hairline while the styling that caused the damage continues means the new hair comes under the same tension as the old. The habit has to change first, or the surgery is treating a symptom."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Children, and starting early"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tight braiding starts young for a lot of Nigerian girls, and a scalp that spends its childhood under tension arrives in adulthood with less to work with. Nothing about a child's hairline should hurt, and a style that makes a child cry going in is too tight regardless of how neat it looks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prevention is unglamorous and it is also the entire treatment for the early stage. Loosen the front, vary where the tension sits between styles, and give the scalp weeks off rather than days."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dry season makes it worse"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hair holds moisture from the air around it, and harmattan takes that moisture away. Drier hair is more brittle hair, and brittle hair snaps under tension it would otherwise survive."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The edges feel this first because those hairs are the finest on your head. A style that sat comfortably in August can be breaking hair at the temples by December without anything about the style having changed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The usual response makes it worse. Reaching for more gel to lay edges that keep lifting adds stiffness and more brushing to hair that is already fragile. Dry months are when to loosen styles and shorten wear time, not when to hold everything down harder."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What to do next"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Photograph your hairline in good light, straight on, and keep it. Comparing two photographs three months apart tells you more than looking in the mirror daily ever will."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thinning at the temples has more than one cause, and they are not treated alike. Tension, thyroid problems, iron deficiency, and inherited pattern loss can look similar at a glance. A consultation with our doctors starts by working out which one you are dealing with, because treating the wrong thing costs you the months that mattered."}]}]}$body_json$::jsonb,
  $body_html$<p>Thinning at the temples and along the hairline, in someone who wears braids, weaves or wigs, is usually traction alopecia — hair loss caused by sustained pulling on the follicle. It can recover while the follicle is still alive, and it stops being reversible once scar tissue takes the follicle's place.</p><p>That single distinction is what the rest of this comes down to. Not which oil, not which product. Whether the follicle is still there.</p><h2>What traction alopecia actually is</h2><p>A hair follicle sits in the scalp with a blood supply feeding it. Pull on the hair hard enough and often enough and you inflame that follicle, and eventually you damage it.</p><p>The pulling doesn't have to be dramatic. It has to be repeated. A style that is slightly too tight, worn for weeks, taken down and immediately replaced with another slightly-too-tight style, does more damage than one painful afternoon ever will.</p><p>This is why the question "are braids bad for your hair?" has no useful answer. Tension, weight, and how long the style stays in are the variables that matter. Braiding itself is not the problem.</p><h2>Why the edges go first</h2><p>The hairs at the temples and along the front hairline are naturally finer and more shallowly anchored than the hair further back. They sit at the outside edge of nearly every style, which is where tension concentrates.</p><p>So the same styling that the rest of your scalp tolerates without complaint can be too much for the perimeter. Loss that starts at the temples and moves backwards in a band is the pattern that points at tension rather than at anything internal.</p><p>A detail worth knowing: a thin line of short, fine hairs is often left standing right at the very front while the hair immediately behind it thins out. It looks like the hairline is still intact. It usually means the opposite.</p><h2>The early signs people ignore</h2><p>The hairline moving is a late sign. Several things show up before it, and they are the ones worth acting on:</p><ul><li><p>Tenderness or a headache for a day or two after a style goes in</p></li><li><p>Small raised bumps or pus-filled spots along the hairline and parting after styling</p></li><li><p>Short, broken hairs at the temples that never seem to get longer</p></li><li><p>Hair in one area that is noticeably finer than the rest, rather than shorter all over</p></li><li><p>Skin at the temples that looks smooth and shiny compared with the scalp elsewhere</p></li></ul><p>That last one matters most. Smooth, shiny skin where you can no longer see the tiny openings of follicles is what scarring looks like, and it changes the whole conversation.</p><p>The bumps are worth taking seriously too. Inflammation around a follicle that is already under mechanical stress adds a second injury on top of the first.</p><h2>The myth that does the most damage</h2><p>Pain during styling is widely treated as proof the work was done properly — that the braids will last, that the parting will stay clean. It is nothing of the sort. Pain is the follicle reporting a load it cannot carry.</p><p>Saying so in the chair is awkward, and plenty of people don't. But a style loosened at the front while it is being put in costs nothing, and it is far cheaper than treatment years later.</p><p>The same applies to adhesives along the hairline, edge gels brushed in with a hard-bristled brush every morning, and any style that has to be slept in without being loosened.</p><h2>Can traction alopecia grow back?</h2><p>While the follicle is alive, yes — if the pulling stops. Hair grows slowly, so this is judged over months. Nobody can give you a timeline before looking at your scalp, and anyone who offers one before examining you is guessing.</p><p>Once the follicle has been replaced by fibrous tissue, no. That is scarring alopecia, and it is permanent in the literal sense: there is no longer a structure there to regrow from. Products cannot reverse it and neither can time.</p><p>Most people who come in are somewhere between the two, with some areas still viable and some not. Working out which is which is the point of an assessment, and it is not something you can do reliably in a mirror.</p><h2>What actually helps</h2><p>Taking the tension off comes first, and nothing else works without it. In practice that means larger sections rather than fine ones at the hairline, lighter extensions, shorter wear time, and a genuine rest period between styles instead of a same-day changeover.</p><p>It also means treating any inflammation on the scalp. A flaking, itchy or bump-prone scalp is a worse environment for a follicle already under stress, and that is treatable on its own.</p><p>Beyond that, the clinic's options depend on what the assessment finds. <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="/treatments/hair-transplant-nigeria">Our hair restoration service</a> covers both the non-surgical treatments used while follicles are still viable and the surgical route for areas that have scarred:</p><ul><li><p>PRP hair restoration — ₦150,000</p></li><li><p>PRF hair restoration — ₦250,000</p></li><li><p>Exosome hair treatment — ₦200,000</p></li><li><p>Dandruff and seborrhoea treatment — ₦120,000</p></li></ul><p>These are current as of July 2026, and a consultation confirms what applies to you. PRP and PRF act on follicles that are shrinking rather than gone, which is exactly why the early window is worth catching — the same treatment does nothing for scarred skin.</p><h2>When it has gone too far</h2><p>For areas that have scarred, a hair transplant is the only way to put hair back. That is a real option and the clinic performs it, but scarred scalp carries a poorer blood supply than healthy scalp, so candidacy is assessed rather than assumed.</p><p>There is also a sequencing problem worth understanding. Transplanting into a hairline while the styling that caused the damage continues means the new hair comes under the same tension as the old. The habit has to change first, or the surgery is treating a symptom.</p><h2>Children, and starting early</h2><p>Tight braiding starts young for a lot of Nigerian girls, and a scalp that spends its childhood under tension arrives in adulthood with less to work with. Nothing about a child's hairline should hurt, and a style that makes a child cry going in is too tight regardless of how neat it looks.</p><p>Prevention is unglamorous and it is also the entire treatment for the early stage. Loosen the front, vary where the tension sits between styles, and give the scalp weeks off rather than days.</p><h2>The dry season makes it worse</h2><p>Hair holds moisture from the air around it, and harmattan takes that moisture away. Drier hair is more brittle hair, and brittle hair snaps under tension it would otherwise survive.</p><p>The edges feel this first because those hairs are the finest on your head. A style that sat comfortably in August can be breaking hair at the temples by December without anything about the style having changed.</p><p>The usual response makes it worse. Reaching for more gel to lay edges that keep lifting adds stiffness and more brushing to hair that is already fragile. Dry months are when to loosen styles and shorten wear time, not when to hold everything down harder.</p><h2>What to do next</h2><p>Photograph your hairline in good light, straight on, and keep it. Comparing two photographs three months apart tells you more than looking in the mirror daily ever will.</p><p>Thinning at the temples has more than one cause, and they are not treated alike. Tension, thyroid problems, iron deficiency, and inherited pattern loss can look similar at a glance. A consultation with our doctors starts by working out which one you are dealing with, because treating the wrong thing costs you the months that mattered.</p>$body_html$,
