  $kt$["Traction alopecia is hair loss caused by sustained pulling on the follicle, most often from braids, weaves, wigs and tight ponytails rather than from any one style.","It can recover while the follicle is still alive. Once the follicle is replaced by scar tissue the loss is permanent, and surgery becomes the only option.","Tenderness, small bumps along the hairline and short broken hairs at the temples are early signs, and they appear well before the hairline visibly moves.","Tension, weight and how long a style stays in matter more than whether you braid at all. Rest periods between styles are what protect the follicle.","Pain during or after styling is not a sign the style was done well. It is a signal to have it loosened.","Assessment matters because thinning at the temples has more than one cause, and the treatments for each are different."]$kt$::jsonb,
  $faq$[{"question":"Can traction alopecia grow back?","answer":"It can, as long as the follicle is still alive and the pulling stops. Recovery is measured in months rather than weeks because hair grows slowly. Once repeated tension has caused scarring, the follicle is gone and hair will not return to that area on its own — which is why the timing of getting it looked at matters more than any single product."},{"question":"How long does it take for edges to grow back?","answer":"Hair grows slowly, so meaningful change is judged over several months, not weeks. The honest answer is that it depends entirely on whether the follicles in that area are still alive, and how much longer they stay under tension. Results vary between people, and nobody can promise a specific timeline before examining your scalp."},{"question":"Do I have to stop braiding my hair completely?","answer":"Usually not. The damage comes from tension, weight and how long a style stays in, rather than from braiding itself. Looser braids, lighter extensions, larger sections, shorter wear time and a rest period between styles all reduce the load on the follicle. What needs to change is specific to your scalp and your styling routine."},{"question":"Is traction alopecia the same as alopecia areata?","answer":"No. Traction alopecia is caused by mechanical pulling on the hair and usually appears where tension is applied, most often the temples and hairline. Alopecia areata is an autoimmune condition that typically causes smooth, round patches of loss anywhere on the scalp. They look different, they are diagnosed differently, and they are not treated the same way."},{"question":"Can wearing a wig cause traction alopecia?","answer":"It can, depending on how the wig is attached. Tight elastic bands, clips gripping the same spot each time, and adhesive applied along the hairline all put repeated stress on the same follicles. Wigs fitted so the weight sits on the scalp rather than pulling on the hairline are far less of a problem."},{"question":"When should I see a doctor about thinning edges?","answer":"When the hairline looks different in photographs a few months apart, when the skin at your temples stays tender or bumpy after styling, or when hair in one area is noticeably shorter and finer than the rest. Earlier is better, because the treatable window closes as scarring develops. Book an assessment rather than waiting to see whether it improves on its own."}]$faq$::jsonb,
  'Traction Alopecia: Why Your Edges Thin and What Helps', 'Thinning edges from braids and weaves is usually traction alopecia. The early signs, when it can still be reversed, and when it cannot.'
